com.automation.sites.listeners.AlterSuiteForCoverageMapListener
# JacocoPerTestCoverageListener (IClassListener + ISuiteListener) — used to be
# added dynamically by AlterSuiteForCoverageMapListener mutating each
# XmlSuite's <listeners> list from inside IAlterSuiteListener.alter(). That
# never actually got the listener wired up in practice (its own onStart/
# onBeforeClass/onAfterClass/onFinish diagnostic prints never showed up in a
# real coverage-map CI run, even though AlterSuiteForCoverageMapListener's
# own "attached" line did — i.e. the alter() call ran, but the listener it
# tried to attach was never invoked), which is why
# Scripts/build-coverage-map.sh kept failing with target/jacoco-per-test
# never being created. Registered here via ServiceLoader instead — the same
# proven-working mechanism already used for the other two listeners on this
# page — so it's always on the classpath, exactly like them. This is safe
# unconditionally: every method on JacocoPerTestCoverageListener already
# checks for the org.jacoco:type=Runtime JMX MBean first and no-ops
# completely unless -Djacoco.jmx=true actually registered it (see its
# javadoc), so an ordinary `mvn test` run is unaffected either way.
# AlterSuiteForCoverageMapListener is still needed and still
# ServiceLoader-registered above — it now only forces parallel="none" on the
# suite when -Dcoverage.map.enabled=true, which per-test coverage capture
# still requires.
com.automation.sites.listeners.JacocoPerTestCoverageListener
# ReportPortal's own TestNG agent (com.epam.reportportal:agent-java-testng —
# see pom.xml's dependency block). Registered here, not via @Listeners or a
# testng-suites/*.xml <listeners> entry, so it covers every suite (web,
# mobile, API) without having to touch 12+ suite XML files by hand — same
# reasoning as AlterSuiteForCoverageMapListener above. Safe to leave
# ServiceLoader-registered on every run: the agent reads rp.enable at
# startup (see reportportal.properties + pom.xml's reportportal.enable
# property, forwarded to the forked JVM the same way coverage.map.enabled
# is) and is a complete no-op — no network calls, nothing written anywhere —
# whenever that resolves to anything other than "true", which is the
# default. Only CI legs that explicitly pass -Dreportportal.enable=true
# (see github-ci.yml) actually report to a ReportPortal server.
com.epam.reportportal.testng.ReportPortalTestNGListener
